+## Revision 26 owner disposition and route design
+
+**Owner direction, 2026-11-21 (Shark decision note 3965).** The review disposition must select the next state through configured workflow routing. For a structured child kickback, the top-level semantic outcome also selects the target child’s configured rework route. A worker-supplied `target_status` is not authoritative; retain it temporarily only as a compatibility assertion and reject it if it disagrees with the resolved route. Do not use the Rider `status set --force` path. Keep the parent lease check around each child write. If an open or answering Question gates a child, pause before child or parent transition.
+
+**Architect consultation.** Shark Rider `consult architect` agrees with this design: reuse the existing outcome router, preserve the parent-owned transition boundary, and fail closed when the child has no configured route or is Question-blocked. The route must be explicit in workflow configuration, including any authorized rework from terminal child states. The owner direction and recommendation are also recorded together in Shark decision note 3965.
+
+**Plan change.** Revision 26 replaces C24’s direct child `status set --force` implementation with configured outcome-based rework. It adds only the `rework_outcomes` route needed for terminal child rework; it does not add a routing service or alter the outer `shark run` interface. The approved revision 25 remains historical evidence and does not authorize development against this revised C24 until its independent review completes.
+
+## Revision 27 route, replay, and transaction design
+
+**Independent review of revision 26: REVISE.** The read-only plan reviewer agreed with the direction and identified six missing contract details: distinguish the configured outcome key from its resolved role; define a dedicated route path for terminal and nonterminal child states; keep GateResult v1 `target_status` required as a compatibility assertion; reconcile applied operations before routing pending work; distinguish fresh-result Question preflight from resume after a durable prefix; retain the parent lease check in the same transaction as child status/history/effects; and scope the removed `status set --force` assertion to the Rider review path while preserving the human escape-hatch test.
+
+**Refined Architect consultation.** The Architect agrees with the refined route contract: `Request.OutcomeKey` (the configured key returned in `recommended_outcome`) selects the child step's `rework_outcomes` destination; the semantic role such as `kickback_rework` is a separate validation value. The destination is resolved from the child's source step, including terminal steps, and is applied through a dedicated routed-rework path rather than a force option. The parent session must be checked inside the child mutation transaction. Replay must reconcile a committed child transition from its operation ID and history before trying to route pending operations.
+
+**Plan change.** Revision 27 adds these distinctions and acceptance evidence to C24 and ADR-002. A fresh result preflights all routes and Question holds before target-store persistence. A resumed result first reconciles its committed prefix, then checks Questions and routes only pending child writes; a later Question or lease loss stops remaining child operations and the parent transition without undoing the authorized prefix. The v1 `target_status` remains mandatory and must match the configured route. Rider removes the legacy free-form kickback command only from the review execution path; unrelated status guidance and the CLI's human `status set --force` escape-hatch coverage remain. The revision 27 plan requires a new independent review before source implementation.
+
+**Independent review of revision 27: APPROVE.** The read-only reviewer confirmed all six revision-26 blockers were addressed and found the route-key/role distinction unambiguous. The reviewer recommended making resume ordering explicit: reconcile committed kickbacks first, then preflight all pending routes and Question holds before applying any pending kickback. C24 and ADR-002 now state this ordering. Source implementation may proceed against revision 27.
