+**Verdict — replan.** Revision 2 addresses the five findings on revision 1. Its required assertions still permit deleting a second task's link, losing the CLI error response during database initialization, and losing two established service and diagnostic behaviors. The earliest affected stage is repair planning. The diagnosis and original baseline remain valid.
+
+**Reviewer** — Codex, independent TechLead for the B078 plan-review dispatch, November 19, 2026. I compared the plan blob in `def42e120ad21bcc4bc3673d1e77e235cd7a4408` with HEAD and the working file. All three have the digest above. Assignment note 3556, Git parent `932dd508^`, the main/branch merge base, and the four historical B078 code reviews independently identify baseline **`bbf490c6ec2bd597d6f3e6e4cdd93e68ca54fa50`**. The parent-context Shark lookup returned research 415, plan 443, evidence 444 and previous review 445. Each of the eight index rows has six cells and links to an existing heading in document 444.
+
+## Open findings
+
+### F1. A second task can lose its link to the target document
+
+Rows 3, 4 and 7 protect the entity type with a bug/task collision and protect the document ID with another document on the target task. Their specified same-type shared document is **564**. The deletion is of **563**. A predicate using only `entity_type` and `document_id` gives the expected result on every specified pair, including the synthetic bug that holds 563.
+
+I compared that predicate with the full three-column predicate using SQLite 3.45.1 and the project DDL. With the four consumer pairs plus `(bug,452,563,specification)`, both deletes removed one row and left identical survivors. Adding `(task,450,563,design)` made the difference observable. The full predicate removed one row. The two-column predicate removed **two**. After correct removal, exact verification returned absent, while verification omitting the parent ID returned present. Either defect can pass the original fixture independently.
+
+Require a same-type, different-ID holder of the **target** in an isolated fixture. After normal deletion and an idempotent retry, assert that its association ID, creation time and link type survive. Retain the existing cross-type and different-document controls. Check mutation and verification independently so dropping any of the three predicates fails. This preserves the existing many-to-many contract. [Association schema and repository requirements](../E21-entity-polymorphism-and-duplication-reduction/E21-F08-polymorphic-data-model-unification/tasks/T-E21-F08-003.md) · [Current repository predicate](../../../internal/repository/entitydoc/repository.go#L73)
+
+### F2. A database initialization panic satisfies the numeric exit check
+
+Row 5 exercises lookup, list, write, verification and commit errors after acquisition of a database handle. The seven factories in row 2 can panic on `GetDB` failure before any of those operations or the command's error wrapper runs.
+
+I built `./cmd/shark` from the delivered B079 commit and ran each of the seven valid selectors against a disposable file containing non-SQLite bytes, with `--json` and an isolated project config. **All seven exited 2 with empty stdout.** Stderr began with a database initialization panic and omitted the requested parent key on its diagnostic line. A post-initialization storage-failure test can pass while a consumer receives no parseable response for this database failure.
+
+Cover acquisition failure after successful selector validation. Require the delete handler to return the wrapped cause, the public operation, label and key, and require the executable to exit 2 with its normal diagnostic in each mode. In JSON mode, parse the error from stdout and check `error=true`, the existing `COMMAND_ERROR` code and the contextual message. In human mode, check the diagnostic on stderr. A panic or success confirmation must fail the assertion. This obligation is local to the seven delete routes. [Service factories](../../../internal/cli/services_global.go#L244) · [Epic and feature factories](../../../internal/cli/service_accessors.go#L232) · [Executable error output](../../../cmd/shark/main.go#L82) · [CLI exit contract](../E07-enhancements/E07-F05-add-related-documents/tasks/T-E07-F05-003.md)
+
+### F3. One unconfigured service cannot establish six independent guards
+
+Row 5 asks for “an unconfigured wrapper.” Epic, feature, task, bug, change-card and tech-debt each implement their own nil guard in `UnlinkDocument`. Each returns `writable document repository not configured` before storage access. The question route calls the generic service directly. Shipped direct unlink tests exercise the epic and feature guards. All seven compiled happy paths use configured services.
+
+Returning nil from the unconfigured task wrapper alone would silently acknowledge the request and pass a representative epic guard test plus all configured CLI checks. Bind the assertion to each of the six wrapper methods, reusing the existing tests. Check the configuration error and absence of storage access at their actual service entry points. [Task guard](../../../internal/services/task_service.go#L1219) · [Tech-debt guard](../../../internal/services/tech_debt_service.go#L515) · [Existing epic test](../../../internal/services/epic_service_test.go#L2459)
+
+### F4. Repeated unlink context passes the diagnostic assertions
+
+Repair step 4 says to wrap the public operation, label and key once. Historical B078 code review r2 F3 identified repeated unlink context. Parent note 3597 records its removal, and note 3625 requires preserving question context. Rows 2 and 5 check for the presence of the label, key and cause. The shipped service and CLI both add unlink context on rejected writes. For a task, the resulting text contains both `unlink document from task <key>` and `failed to unlink document from task <key>`. The current B078 regression counts only the latter string. Its count is still one.
+
+At the rejected-write boundary, require one occurrence of the operation and parent context while retaining the cause, public label, key and exit status. Include the task and the shared dispatcher, where the public labels `change-card` and `tech-debt` differ from the stored kinds. Preserve the question handler's required wrapping check. This is a gap in proof of a disposition already accepted on B078. [B078 regression](../../../tests/contracts/b078_related_docs_delete_test.go#L129) · [Shipped service wrapping](https://github.com/jwwelbor/shark-task-manager/blob/c0a66d2fe9295107527a2c00f80933dabaedbca6/internal/services/entity_document_service.go#L146) · [Shipped CLI wrapping](https://github.com/jwwelbor/shark-task-manager/blob/c0a66d2fe9295107527a2c00f80933dabaedbca6/internal/cli/commands/related_docs.go#L206)
+
+## Coverage and prior findings
+
+| Plan row | Independent assessment |
+|---|---|
+| 1. Parent selection | The specified empty/valid flag combinations catch first-selector-wins. The DB-file check distinguishes validation before service access. Shipped add/list share the resolver and preserve positional list inference. |
+| 2. Seven routes | The compiled association and output checks distinguish an omitted or mislabelled route. The expected labels, in flag order, are epic, feature, task, bug, change-card, question and tech-debt. The stored exceptions are change and tech_debt. F2 and F4 cover failure paths. |
+| 3. Document identity | Assigning the observed legacy winner to another parent in both path orderings detects a global lookup. Exact title matching, immediate ID/path readback and ambiguity with zero mutations distinguish wrong selection from absence. F1 covers the additional parent-ID control. |
+| 4. Committed absence | My independent SQLite probe gave affected/present counts of 1/0 for removal, 0/1 for an ignored delete, 1/1 for statement-local restoration and 0/0 for absence. Checking the exact triple catches both retained cases. F1 covers a verification query with an incomplete predicate. |
+| 5. Failure semantics | Numeric process exits 0/1/2 distinguish the known outcomes. The handler-level question sentinel check rejects a conversion from wrapping to string interpolation. F2–F4 identify remaining assertions. |
+| 6. Tech-debt client | A service-only implementation, missing flag, untrimmed padded slug, wrong public label, global title lookup or discarded slug-lookup failure each breaks a named requirement. Reuse PR #285 contracts and its positional/explicit readbacks on the integrated candidate. |
+| 7. WWGM workaround | Checks of the four actual owner pairs, paths and bytes detect deletion of 542 or either 564 link. The synthetic same-type holder in F1 belongs in a separate fixture. |
+| 8. Evidence and invalidation | The index distinguishes unit doubles, isolated compiled contracts and candidate-bound review and QA. Both legacy winner orderings expose the historical hard-coded path expectations. Retain the TC-067 skip and the candidate limits on prior results. |
+
+All five revision 1 counterexamples now have explicit responses. Rows 1/2/6 cover the shipped tech-debt route, row 5 covers numeric exits and question error identity, and row 4 covers restored rows and cross-type collisions. The new findings identify distinct ways required behavior can still be lost. The prior review and its original findings remain linked. [Revision 1 review](B078.plan-review-r1.md) · [B078 historical code reviews and dispositions](B078.repair-plan-evidence.md#reviews)
+
