+# B078 independent repair plan review, revision 3
+
+plan_path: docs/plan/bugs/B078.repair-plan.md
+plan_sha256: sha256:135df708c16987224ea6c41fe641232de733ee324ed6fb19945416072d236b6a
+
+**Verdict — replan.** Revision 3 addresses all nine findings from the two previous plan reviews. Three coverage gaps remain. The shipped change-card slug lookup loses a database error. The proposed repository transaction also needs assertions for validation before storage access and for caller cancellation. Repair planning is the earliest affected stage. The WWGM diagnosis and the original review baseline still hold.
+
+**Reviewer and revision.** Codex, independent TechLead for the B078 plan-review dispatch, November 19, 2026. I reviewed the plan blob at `269a533fccda77b73df84ddf099a5f5a06ab71a3`. Its SHA-256 matches the working file and Shark note 3844. Assignment note 3556, the Git parent of first repair commit `932dd508`, the merge base with the inspected `origin/main`, and the baseline printed in all four historical B078 code reviews independently agree on **`bbf490c6ec2bd597d6f3e6e4cdd93e68ca54fa50`**. The B079 delivery is a dependency. The assigned project’s related-document list returned research 415, plan 443, mutable evidence 444 and prior reviews 445 and 446. I checked all twelve index rows for the six required columns and resolved every evidence link to a heading in document 444.
+
+## Open findings
+
+### F1. A slugged change-card key can hide a database error
+
+Repair step 5 requires preserving lookup failures at their source. Row 5 requires a lookup failure. Row 6 specifically challenges the tech-debt slug fallback. The change-card fallback is a second implementation with the same defect. After the exact-key query misses, `ChangeCardRepository.GetByKey` discards a scan error from its key-and-slug query and returns `repoerr.ErrNotFound`. The registered change-card service uses this repository through its entity adapter. A fix to the tech-debt fallback and the common exit classifier passes the specified cases while this route still emits exit 1 for an operational failure. [Change-card lookup at the shipped revision](https://github.com/jwwelbor/shark-task-manager/blob/c0a66d2fe9295107527a2c00f80933dabaedbca6/internal/repository/changecard/repository.go#L94-L130) · [CLI exit contract](../E07-enhancements/E07-F05-add-related-documents/tasks/T-E07-F05-003.md)
+
+I built the executable from the shipped B079 Git tree and used a disposable SQLite database. Before changing the fixture, I listed the linked report through `--change=CC-001-fixture`. I then set the fixture's nullable integer `priority` to SQL NULL. These deletes used the same executable and database.
+
+| Selector | Observed JSON diagnostic | Exit |
+|---|---|---|
+| `--change=CC-001` | Identified a scan failure converting the NULL priority to an integer | 1 |
+| `--change=CC-001-fixture` | Reported the existing change card missing and lost the scan cause | 1 |
+| `--change=CC-999-fixture` | Reported a genuinely absent change card | 1 |
+
+The association survived all three calls. Require the actual registered change-card delete to distinguish these cases. On the two failed reads of the existing card, require exit 2, the underlying scan cause, the public operation and supplied key, no confirmation and the retained association. For the absent card require exit 1. At the repository boundary keep the scan error identifiable and reserve the not-found sentinel for a genuine miss. The healthy slug control proves the fixture reaches the fallback. Retain row 6's separate tech-debt case.
+
+### F2. Starting a transaction can mask the repository’s input errors
+
+The E21-F08 data model requires a recognized entity type and a positive parent ID. T-E21-F08-003 AC-10 and EC-2 require validation before SQL. Its architecture review, signed by the Architect Agent, explicitly verified the pre-SQL type guard. The shipped `EntityDocumentRepository.Unlink` enforces both guards. Rows 4, 5 and 9 use valid repository arguments. Row 11 covers an unset service dependency, one layer earlier. The existing repository tests use an available database and inspect the returned errors. Beginning a transaction before validating, then returning the correct validation error when begin succeeds, passes those tests. An unavailable database exposes the ordering change. [Architecture approval and repository requirements](../E21-entity-polymorphism-and-duplication-reduction/E21-F08-polymorphic-data-model-unification/tasks/T-E21-F08-003.md) · [E21 data model](../E21-entity-polymorphism-and-duplication-reduction/E21-F08-polymorphic-data-model-unification/tech/03-data-models.md) · [Existing repository tests](../../../internal/repository/entitydoc/repository_test.go#L319)
+
+I called the shipped repository directly against a closed database. Type `project` with ID 452 returned `invalid entity type: "project"`. Type `task` with IDs 0 and −1 returned `entity_id must be positive, got 0` and `entity_id must be positive, got -1` respectively. Valid `task,452,563` reached storage and returned `sql: database is closed` in its error chain. Require the existing diagnostics at this boundary for invalid type, zero ID and negative ID with zero transaction or query attempts. A valid-argument control must reach the instrumented storage failure. Reuse the existing test cases where they cover the same inputs.
+
+### F3. The transaction checks allow a delete that ignores caller cancellation
+
+Repair step 4 specifies a context-bound transaction. Rows 4 and 5 exercise storage outcomes and independently injected begin, execution, verification and commit failures. Those assertions can all pass with a live background context substituted for the caller throughout the operation. The shipped repository passes the caller’s context into its write. The project Go rules and coding standards explicitly require propagating context for cancellation. [Production repository](../../../internal/repository/entitydoc/repository.go#L65) · [Context rules](../../../.claude/rules/go/patterns.md#context-usage) · [Coding standards](../../architecture/coding-standards.md)
+
+With an existing `(task,452,563)` association, a direct call on an already-cancelled context returned an error matching `context.Canceled`. A fresh database reader counted one retained association. Repeating the same call with a live context succeeded and the fresh reader counted zero. Require that paired observation at the changed repository entry point. This distinguishes cancellation propagation from injected database errors and catches a detached transaction that deletes the link after its caller cancels.
+
+## Coverage assessment
+
+| Row | Independent counterfactual and disposition |
+|---|---|
+| 1. Parent selection | A first-flag-wins implementation fails the mixed empty/nonempty cases. All seven registrations and the pre-storage validation check cover dispatch, including trimmed and malformed tech-debt keys. Shipped add/list contracts cover the shared selector and positional list behavior. |
+| 2. Routes and labels | The stored association and both outputs detect wrong dispatch and wrong label independently. Flag order maps to epic, feature, task, bug, change-card, question and tech-debt. Stored kinds for the two differing labels are `change` and `tech_debt`. |
+| 3. Document identity | Both observed-winner assignments, exact title matching, case-variant retention, ambiguity with zero writes, and immediate ID/path readback distinguish a global lookup, wrong-ID deletion or deletion of document data. |
+| 4. Committed absence | My in-memory probe with shipped SQLite DDL produced affected/present pairs of 1/0 for normal removal, 0/1 for an ignored delete, 1/1 for restoration by an AFTER trigger and 0/0 for absence. Checking affected rows alone misclassifies a retained link. F2 and F3 cover other existing repository behaviors during the transaction change. |
+| 5. Error classification | Process exits 0/1/2, stage-specific errors, rollback checks and the question RunE sentinel distinguish swallowed writes, generic exit 1 and lost wrapping. F1 identifies the second slug fallback. F2 and F3 identify inputs that change which error the repository must return. |
+| 6. Tech-debt caller | The compiled numeric, slugged and padded keys exercise its own lookup, trimming, ambiguity and storage errors. The separate entity’s same-title link must survive deletion and retry. This caller’s fallback test cannot reach the change-card repository. |
+| 7. WWGM consumer | Checking B107→542, T013→563/564 and T011→564 individually detects unlinking the wrong ID or either workaround holder. The actual owner paths and file existence match the fixture requirements. |
+| 8. Candidate evidence | Binding both legacy-winner permutations, real executable tests, isolated unit doubles and the ordered Go gate to one integrated candidate rejects stale fixture results. Keep the historical TC-067 skip and B079 QA’s full-suite omission with their original runs. |
+| 9. Three-part key | I removed each predicate independently. Each shortened DELETE removed two rows. The complete DELETE removed one and preserved all three siblings byte-for-byte at the SQL column level. After correct removal, every shortened existence query returned present and the full query returned absent. |
+| 10. DB initialization | I reproduced exit 2, empty JSON stdout and panic on stderr on all seven shipped selectors against an invalid disposable DB. Requiring a handler cause, no later call and the actual structured or human diagnostic rejects that false pass. |
+| 11. Unconfigured wrappers | All six public wrappers have their own guard. Direct checks of the exact configuration error and zero storage accesses detect a single wrapper silently succeeding. Question uses the generic service directly and is covered in row 5. |
+| 12. Rejected-write diagnostic | Counting the bare operation, supplied key and complete public phrase across the message catches the historical nested wrapping even when the old task-specific substring count is one. Whole-label checks distinguish the change-card and tech-debt public names. |
+
+## Authorities and retained evidence
+
+Shark records E07-F05, T-E07-F05-003, E21-F08, E39-F01 and B072 as completed. E07-F05 returned an empty related-document list. Its PRD and CLI task explicitly supply association-only removal, repeat-delete behavior, confirmation and the exit codes. E21-F08 supplies the polymorphic identity and validation rules. T-E21-F08-003 is in development and has a dated architecture approval for its repository contract. E39-F01 supplies the generic question document path. The baseline service’s global title lookup and five CLI branches that discard errors support the research diagnosis. The shipped command uses six public service wrappers and one direct generic question caller. Production call-site search found no HTTP unlink route. [Research](B078.research-report.md) · [E07 requirements](../E07-enhancements/E07-F05-add-related-documents/prd.md) · [Question registration](../E39-question-and-decision-workflow-management/E39-F01-question-entity-and-platform-registration/spec.md)
+
+Shark parent `codex-parent` authorized selector cardinality and isolated compiled-CLI integration tests in B078/3603. B078/3597 and 3625 establish the diagnostic and duplicate-title fixture dispositions. I checked the original B078 r1–r4 reports, both prior plan reviews, and the B079 research, initial review, r7/r8/r10 and QA records. B079/3572, 3606, 3611 and 3612 require shared selection, operational errors, trimmed keys, blank-key rejection and typed kinds. GitHub confirms owner `jwwelbor` merged reviewed head `9206cc18eb14ab10f469bb1f5042b609beb53941` as `c0a66d2fe9295107527a2c00f80933dabaedbca6`. The relevant caller, repository and B079 contract files match inspected upstream `f4b03228fa1a71efe295ecc051d8fd9f5dc6e40d`. That delivery supersedes B072’s former tech-debt exclusion and the earlier permission to keep separate selectors. The parent’s selector decision supersedes E07’s optional interactive sketch. B079’s catalog getter has its own command and evidence. [Review ledger](B078.repair-plan-evidence.md#reviews) · [Revision 1 review](B078.plan-review-r1.md) · [Revision 2 review](B078.plan-review-r2.md) · [PR 285](https://github.com/jwwelbor/shark-task-manager/pull/285)
+
+In WWGM I read the T013 task, the architect’s integration decision and predecessor scope/qualification briefs, the TD-595 compatibility decision, and their reports 560/562/565/569. I checked owner notes 3031 and 3039–3043 and the four actual associations through a read-only database connection. The historical query projection returned 542 first. Both root-file pointers resolve and the task-relative 563 path is missing. Neither original invocation recorded the selected ID. Decision 3043 supersedes the removal claim in 3041, identifies 564 as the valid workaround and assigns the defect to Shark. The WWGM architect’s 16-file composition, bounded retries, performance budgets, provider limits and D02 gate apply to that project. The reviewed Shark unlink path has no model selector, provider invocation or billing field. B078/3562 and 3569 document a dispatch-specific provider decision. No required owner source or disposition was unavailable. [Integration decision](/home/jwwel/projects/wwgm/docs/plan/decisions/DB-2026-10-15-review-tool-integration.md) · [Compatibility decision](/home/jwwel/projects/wwgm/docs/plan/decisions/DB-2026-10-15-research-cli-compatibility.md)
+
+**Handoff.** Return B078 to repair planning for F1–F3. Keep the original baseline, consumer evidence, nine prior findings and their revision-specific dispositions. A material change requires a new committed plan digest and independent review. The probes above characterize shipped behavior and proposed test fixtures. Implementation evidence, candidate review and QA remain due against the eventual approved snapshot and integrated candidate.
